text:
Maya: Quick question for the runtime folks — I'm thinking we should shrink the public "opaque backing" API that our private deployments consume so operators can swap in different backing implementations (NVMe paging, remote hostpool, vendor HBM proxy) without touching allocator internals. The current surface is pretty large and leaks too many flags into the allocator. Thoughts?

Santiago: I like the idea. The allocator cares mostly about two things: a) a page/granule handle you can map/unmap fast, b) a minimal set of lifecycle ops (pin/release/evict hint). Everything else can be async behind the backing.

Jo: big +1. Right now the allocator assumes backing gives pages with a bunch of metadata (stripe id, location, compressibility). That coupling makes refactors painful. If we make the backing return an opaque token + small trait flags, we can move logic into the backing layer.

Rachel: question: how do we handle residency queries? alloc currently calls `is_resident(handle)` a lot. If handle is opaque we either add a cheap residency bit or introduce an async residency check path which could add latency.

Santiago: proposal: two sync ops and one async hint: `acquire_page(token) -> mapped_ptr|error`, `handback_page(token, reason_flags)`, `prefetch_hint(token, deadline_ms)`. Residency becomes "mapped_ptr != NULL" for hot paths. If backing needs to do a lookup it can use a small residency bit stored in page table (fast CAS-read).

Maya: sketch of the tiny API (pseudo-C):
```
// Opaque handle returned by backing on alloc
struct BackingHandle { uint64_t id; uint8_t kind; };

// Sync: get CPU-accessible pointer (may trigger immediate staging)
int backing_acquire(BackingHandle h, void** out_ptr);

// Sync: release/handback; reason_flags e.g. EVICT, COMPACT, MIGRATE
int backing_handback(BackingHandle h, uint32_t reason_flags);

// Async hint: optional, best-effort
void backing_prefetch_hint(BackingHandle h, uint32_t deadline_ms);
```

Jo: Sounds good. A few tradeoffs: 1) an extra call per page could add overhead for tiny allocations, 2) we need a fast path where the allocator keeps a small cache of mapped_ptrs for recent handles, 3) error handling semantics (what does acquire return when remote fetch fails?)

Santiago: for (1) we keep a small inline mapping cache in the allocator's hot path (like a direct map of last N handles) and prefer `backing_acquire` to be inlined via function pointer to avoid syscall overhead. For (3) acquire should return EAGAIN/EIO and allocator must have retry/backoff + fallback to a compact/evict path. We should not block request threads indefinitely.

Rachel: we also need accounting hooks. If backing does staging to host memory or NVMe it should report cost units so billing + cost-based routing can do the right thing. Maybe `backing_handback` returns staged_bytes and staging_cost_tag? or we emit structured telemetry from backing.

Maya: agreed — keep the API small but allow structured telemetry out-of-band (emit event with handle id). The goal here is interface contraction, not losing observability.

Jo: migration plan: implement shim that implements new tiny API over the old backing (translate old metadata -> handle), land allocator to use new API behind a feature flag, and then refactor backings one-by-one. That should keep rolling rollouts safe.

Santiago: one more thought — for long-lived pinned pages we should allow a "pin_with_owner" variant so the backing can avoid expensive tracking if the allocator promises to drive handback. That reduces bookkeeping cost for hot pinned pages.

Maya: good. I'll write up a small follow-up RFC with the API sketch, the shim compatibility plan, and perf microbench targets (acquire < 200ns on hot path, cache hit > 95%). I'll ping infra for perf runs.

infra-bot: perf-ci: queued microbench job #7822 for branch maya/opaque-backing. Results will post in this thread. :robot_face:

Rachel: nits: add explicit error codes for transient vs permanent failures and a small SLA doc for backing implementations (max staging latency we tolerate).

Maya: noted. Thanks all — I'll draft RFC and link here. @Santiago @Jo if you can review quickly that would be great. :thumbsup:
